s seat wind is North Wind (F3 is East, South, West, North? Wait, F1 is East, F2 South, F3 West, F4 North. So F3 is West Wind. The user's seat wind is North (F4). So having F3 (West) is not their seat wind. \n\nThe prevailing wind is East (F1). So if the user is going for a Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind pung, F3 isn't relevant. \n\nTherefore, F3 is just a regular wind tile. \n\nGiven that, discarding F3 is probably safe. \n\nSo, the best move is to discard F3. \n\nAlternatively, the user could discard B9, but that's a terminal. \n\nWait, the user's hand has B3 (2), B5, B9. If they discard B9, they still have B3 and B5. Maybe they can form a B3-B4-B5 chow if they get B4. \n\nBut if they discard B9, they lose that terminal. \n\nBut if they keep B9, they might be able to use it for a terminal pattern. \n\nHowever, without more terminals, it's unlikely. \n\nGiven that, maybe discarding F3 is better. \n\nSo, the answer would be to play F3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard F3 as it is a single honor tile not part of any potential chow or pung, and discarding it minimizes disruption to the user's hand while considering the availability of remaining F3 tiles.\nReason: F3 is a West Wind tile, not the user's seat wind (North) or the prevailing wind (East). It doesn't contribute to any immediate chow or pung in the current hand. Discarding it preserves other tiles critical for forming chows (e.g., W4-W5, T3-T4, T7-T8) and terminals (B9), while reducing the risk of others completing their hands with discarded honors.\nAnswer: Play F3"}
