es that could form pairs. For example, B3 (2 copies). If I keep both B3s, maybe I can form a pair later. But since I'm discarding, maybe it's better to get rid of one to reduce the chance of others chi-ing.\n\nIn conclusion, the safest tile to discard might be B3, since it's part of a possible chi but the chances are lower given the unshown tiles. Also, I have another B3, so losing one isn't too bad. Alternatively, playing T7 or T8 might be risky if someone can chi. But between the options, B3 seems safer.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The best move is to discard B3, as it minimizes the risk of enabling others to form melds while maintaining flexibility in my hand.]\nReason: [B3 is part of a possible chi (B2-B3-B4 or B3-B4-B5), but B2 has only 1 unshown tile left, reducing its utility for others. Discarding one B3 leaves another in hand for potential pung or pair. Other options like T7/T8 risk aiding chows in the bamboo suit (T6-T7-T8), which the previous player may be pursuing.]\nAnswer: Play B3"}
