8, T9, B4, B5, B6, J2. The J2 is a single tile, so maybe it's part of a pair or a Pung. But there's only one J2. The B4, B5, B6 are in sequence, which could be a Chow in the B suit. So if I have those three, that's a possible meld. But again, I need to see if I can form that. Wait, I have one of each, so if I can form a Chi on B4-5-6, that would be good. But again, that requires someone else to discard one of those tiles. Alternatively, maybe I can keep them for a concealed meld.\n\nLet me think about the scoring patterns. My current hand has a Chi of W5-6-7. If I can form another Chi, like T7-8-9, and B4-5-6, that would give me three Chows, plus the existing one. Wait, no, the existing one is already a meld. So if I form two more Chows, that would make three Chows plus the pair. But I need four melds and a pair. Let me count:\n\nCurrent meld: W5-6-7 (a Chi). Then, if I form T7-8-9 (another Chi), and B4-5-6 (another Chi), that would be three Chows. Plus the pair would need to come from the remaining tiles. Let's see: my remaining tiles after those three Chows would be W4, W8, W9, J2. So the pair would need to be two of these. But I have one W4, two W8s, one W9, and one J2. The pair could be W8-W8. So if I can form three Chows (W5-6-7, T7-8-9, B4-5-6) and a pair of W8s, that would be a winning hand. That would be a hand with four melds (three Chows and one pair). But I need to check if that's possible.\n\nWait, the hand has 14 tiles. Let's count:\n\n- W5, W6, W7 (3)\n- T7, T8, T9 (3)\n- B4, B5, B6 (3)\n- W8, W8 (2)\n- J2 (1)\n\nWait, that's 3+3+3+2+1=12 tiles. Hmm, something's wrong. Oh, right, the initial hand has 14 tiles. The existing meld is already part of the hand. So when I discard a tile, I'm left with 13 tiles. Wait, maybe I'm getting confused here. Let me recheck.\n\nOriginal hand: 14 tiles. The meld of W5, W6, W7 is three tiles, so the remaining hand is 11 tiles. If I form another two Chows (each three tiles), that would be 3+3=6, plus a pair (2 tiles), to